> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::ml;
  double worst = 0;
  int checked = 0;
  for (auto f :
       {m::GlmFamily::kPoisson, m::GlmFamily::kGamma, m::GlmFamily::kNegativeBinomial})
    for (double value : {-2., -1., 0., 1., 2.}) {
      auto eta = n::scalar(value, n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
      const auto y = n::scalar(2, n::DType::Float64);
      const double g = n::grad(m::GlmNll(eta, y, f, 2), {eta})[0].item();
      const double mu = std::exp(value), exact = f == m::GlmFamily::kPoisson ? mu - 2
                                                 : f == m::GlmFamily::kGamma
                                                     ? 2 * (1 - 2 / mu)
                                                     : 2 * (mu - 2) / (2 + mu);
      worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(g - exact));
      ++checked;
    }
  if (worst > 1e-10)
    return 1;
  std::print("{} analytic score probes; maximum discrepancy {:.12f}.\n", checked, worst);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
15 analytic score probes; maximum discrepancy 0.000000000000.
// result_end


> Fitting means with a tensor optimizer

The intercept-only training responses 1,2,3,2 have mean 2. For each fixed dispersion,
the score sums to zero at eta=log(2). The optimizer fits eta directly; this analytic
answer checks the fit rather than supplying its parameters.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::ml;
  const auto dtype = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto y = n::tensor({1, 2, 3, 2}, {4}, dtype);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  std::vector<std::string> family;
  std::vector<double> fitted, error, nll;
  int index = 0;
  for (auto kind :
       {m::GlmFamily::kPoisson, m::GlmFamily::kGamma, m::GlmFamily::kNegativeBinomial}) {
    auto intercept = n::scalar(0, dtype).requires_grad_();
    n::OptimizerConfig config;
    config.kind = n::OptimizerKind::Adam;
    n::Optimizer optimizer({intercept}, .05, config);
    for (int step = 0; step < 800; ++step) {
      const auto loss = n::mean(m::GlmNll(intercept + n::zeros_like(y), y, kind, 2));
      optimizer.minimize(loss);
    }
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    fitted.push_back(n::exp(intercept).item());
    error.push_back(std::abs(fitted.back() - 2));
    nll.push_back(n::mean(m::GlmNll(intercept + n::zeros_like(y), y, kind, 2)).item());
    family.push_back(index == 0 ? "Poisson" : index == 1 ? "gamma" : "negative binomial");
    ++index;
    if (error.back() > 1e-8)
      return 1;
  }
  table.add_column("family", family);
  table.add_column("fitted_mean", fitted);
  table.add_column("analytic_mean_error", error);
  table.add_column("mean_NLL", nll);
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(9));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| family | fitted_mean | analytic_mean_error | mean_NLL |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Poisson | 2 | 0 | 1.408219096 |
| gamma | 2 | 0 | 1.378773338 |
| negative binomial | 2 | 0 | 1.703422192 |
// result_end

> Conclusion

Analytic scores agree with differentiation across all three fixed-dispersion likelihoods.
A positive mean function does not make the distributions interchangeable. Response support,
dispersion choice and held-out likelihood should be assessed before interpreting fitted coefficients.

> References

- [Stan Functions Reference: distributions](https://mc-stan.org/docs/functions-reference/unbounded_discrete_distributions.html).
- [R family documentation and link conventions](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/family.html).
